@{
    Layout = "~/Views/Shared/_Layout.cshtml";
    ViewBag.Title = "Fanex Grid in a nutshell";
}
<div class="col-sm-12">
    <h4> 1) Simple Example</h4>

    <p>
        We start the series of tutorial by very simple example. There are three steps to complete this example.
    </p>
    <p>
        <b>First</b>, we need a container to display the Grid.
    </p>

<pre class="brush: javascript">
<div id="gridId" class="freegrid"></div>
</pre>
    <p>
        The container must have ID, this ID must unique for each container, and has "freegrid" class.
    </p>
    <p>
        <b>
            Second
        </b>, we need a Grid option, which will describe everything display on the Gird. Let’s see a simple example about Grid Option:
    </p>

<pre class="brush: javascript">
//// Randomize Data
function generateData(numberRows) {
   var rows = [];
   for (var index = 0; index < numberRows; index++) {
     rows.push(
       {
            Columns: [
                { Name: "No", Value: index * 2 },
                { Name: "Text1", Value: (Math.random() > 0.5 ? (Math.random() * 500).toFixed(3) : (Math.random() * 500 * -1).toFixed(3)) },
                { Name: "Number1", Value: (Math.random() * 20000).toFixed(4) },
                { Name: "Number2", Value: (Math.random() * 100).toFixed(2) },
                { Name: "Number3", Value: (Math.random() * 500).toFixed(3) },
                { Name: "Text2", Value: Math.random().toString(36).substring(7) },
                { Name: "Text3", Value: Math.random().toString(36).substring(8) },
                { Name: "Text4", Value: Math.random().toString(36).substring(9) },
                { Name: "Number4", Value: (Math.random() * 1000).toFixed(3) },
                { Name: "Number5", Value: (index % 2 == 0 ? (Math.random() * 500).toFixed(3) : (Math.random() * 500 * -1).toFixed(3)) }
           ]
      }
    );
  }
  return rows;
}
var gridOption = {
   columns: [
      { HeaderText: "Header 1", Width: 60, Name: "No", HeaderAlign: "center", CellAlign: "center"},
      { HeaderText: "Header 2", Width: 70, Name: "Text1", HeaderAlign: "center", CellAlign: "right" },
      { HeaderText: "Header 3", Width: 100, Name: "Number1", HeaderAlign: "center", CellAlign: "right" },
      { HeaderText: "Header 4", Width: 100, Name: "Number2", HeaderAlign: "center", CellAlign: "right" },
      { HeaderText: "Header 5", Width: 160, Name: "Number3", HeaderAlign: "center", CellAlign: "right" },
      { HeaderText: "Header 6", Width: 110, Name: "Text2" },
      { HeaderText: "Header 7", Width: 120, Name: "Text3" },
      { HeaderText: "Header 8", Width: 150, Name: "Text4" },
      { HeaderText: "Header 9", Width: 90, Name: "Number4", HeaderAlign: "center", CellAlign: "right" },
      { HeaderText: "Header 10", Width: 120, Name: "Number5", HeaderAlign: "center", CellAlign: "right" }
   ],
   bodyRows: generateData(100)
}
</pre>
    <p>
        <text class="text-danger">The "columns" property describes everything about Grid Columns</text> and three required properties are Width, Name, HeaderText, and Name is primary key for each column, this property contains all general information about columns form header, body to footer.
    </p>
    <p>
        <text class="text-danger">The "bodyRows" property describes everything will display on the Grid body</text>, and each row will have a list of column map one by one with column of "columns". We can understand that is the way we bind data source. See image below for more details:
    </p>
    <p>
        Note:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <text class="text-danger">All Properties of DataSource will name follow to the Pascal Case Convention</text> (for binding easily from server code return JSON).
        </li>
        <li>
            <text class="text-danger">All Grid Options will name follow to Camel Case Convention.</text>
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        <img src="~/Content/Images/Part2Example1.png" />
    </p>
    <p>
        <b>Last step</b>, We start render.
    </p>

<pre class="brush: javascript">
    $("#gridId").fanexGrid(gridOption);
</pre>
    <p>
        Ok, you did it, and the result is:
    </p>
    <p>
        <img width="100%" src="~/Content/Images/Part2Example2.png" />
    </p>
    <p>
        So we just created successfully a Grid using "Fanex.Grid".
    </p>
    <h4>
        2) Understand two ways config Grid Height
    </h4>
    <p>
        <b>a) Auto height</b>
    </p>
    <p>
        To set Grid auto height, we declare <text class="text-danger">"gridExpandHeight"</text> , and set it equal with distance from top of current Window to top of Grid. The Fanex.Grid will auto calculate Grid height by take the Height of current Window and subtract to <text class="text-danger">"gridExpandHeight"</text> then auto re-size Grid, if <text class="text-danger">"gridExpandHeight"</text> greater than distance from top of current Window to top of Grid, Fanex.Grid auto expand bottom of Grid. See image below:
    </p>
    <p>
        <img width="100%" src="~/Content/Images/Part2Example3.png" />
    </p>
    <p>
        <b>b) Configurable Height:</b>
    </p>
    <p>
        To use configurable height, we have to set <text class="text-danger">"fullHeight" property to "false"</text> and then set <text class="text-danger">"gridHeight" property to value</text> that we need. See example below:
    </p>
    <img src="~/Content/Images/Part2Example4.png" />
</div>